WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.299
This lesson becomes much clearer when stability is treated as a support-geometry story.

2
00:00:05.299 --> 00:00:10.598
What matters is where the line of action through the center of mass, called the Balance Core line in this module, lands relative to the base.

3
00:00:10.598 --> 00:00:15.897
If that line stays inside the support zone, the object can remain stable.

4
00:00:15.897 --> 00:00:21.196
If it moves beyond the edge, tipping begins.

5
00:00:21.196 --> 00:00:26.495
So the core reasoning is about margins and geometry, not just about how heavy the object sounds.

6
00:00:26.495 --> 00:00:31.794
Keep this takeaway in view: Stability is judged by where the weight line lands relative to the support base.

7
00:00:31.794 --> 00:00:37.093
Balance Core means center-of-mass line.

8
00:00:37.093 --> 00:00:42.392
The weight line drops through the object's balance point.

9
00:00:42.392 --> 00:00:47.691
Footprint Zone means support base.

10
00:00:47.691 --> 00:00:52.990
This is the area that keeps the object supported.

11
00:00:52.990 --> 00:00:59.098
This balance-core diagram shows two support cases.

12
00:00:59.098 --> 00:01:05.205
In the narrow support, the Balance Core line lands close to the edge, so only a small extra shift is needed before tipping begins.

13
00:01:05.205 --> 00:01:11.312
In the wide support, the same load position leaves a much larger margin before the line reaches the edge.

14
00:01:11.312 --> 00:01:17.420
That is why the wider platform is more stable.

15
00:01:17.420 --> 00:01:23.528
The line location and the remaining margin are the main story, not the weight label by itself.

16
00:01:23.528 --> 00:01:29.635
Keep this takeaway in view: The tipping margin, not just the weight, is what stability reasoning tracks.

17
00:01:29.635 --> 00:01:35.742
Narrow support: Small margin before the Balance Core line reaches the edge.

18
00:01:35.742 --> 00:01:41.850
Wide support: Larger margin before tipping can begin.

19
00:01:41.850 --> 00:01:48.024
Imagine carrying a tall stack on a tray.

20
00:01:48.024 --> 00:01:54.198
If the tray is narrow, the balance line can reach the edge with only a small sideways shift.

21
00:01:54.198 --> 00:02:00.372
If the tray is wider, the same stack has more room before that happens.

22
00:02:00.372 --> 00:02:06.546
And if the stack is lower, it is generally harder to tip because the line is easier to keep over the base.

23
00:02:06.546 --> 00:02:12.720
The analogy matters because it turns stability into a support and margin problem rather than a mysterious label.

24
00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:18.894
Keep this takeaway in view: Stability changes when the support width or the center-of-mass height changes, even if the load stays the same.

25
00:02:18.894 --> 00:02:25.068
Wider tray means more stable.

26
00:02:25.068 --> 00:02:31.242
The support zone is larger, so the line can move more before tipping.

27
00:02:31.242 --> 00:02:37.416
Taller stack means less stable.

28
00:02:37.416 --> 00:02:43.590
A higher center of mass makes tipping easier when disturbed.

29
00:02:43.590 --> 00:02:48.698
A stable object is one whose Balance Core line still falls within the Footprint Zone.

30
00:02:48.698 --> 00:02:53.807
The farther inside the line lands, the larger the safety margin before tipping begins.

31
00:02:53.807 --> 00:02:58.915
A wider base improves this margin.

32
00:02:58.915 --> 00:03:04.023
Lowering the center of mass also helps because disturbances are less likely to push the line outside the base.

33
00:03:04.023 --> 00:03:09.132
This is why stability can be improved in more than one way: by widening the support, lowering the load, or repositioning the mass distribution.

34
00:03:09.132 --> 00:03:14.240
Keep this takeaway in view: Stability reasoning is about line placement, support width, and tipping margin.

35
00:03:14.240 --> 00:03:19.348
Line inside base means stable condition.

36
00:03:19.348 --> 00:03:24.457
Support still exists under the weight line.

37
00:03:24.457 --> 00:03:29.565
More margin means more stable.

38
00:03:29.565 --> 00:03:34.673
A bigger inside distance from line to edge means more tipping resistance.

39
00:03:34.673 --> 00:03:39.782
Check whether the Balance Core line is inside the base.

40
00:03:39.782 --> 00:03:44.890
Compare how much margin remains before the edge.

41
00:03:44.890 --> 00:03:50.376
Heavier does not automatically mean more stable.

42
00:03:50.376 --> 00:03:55.862
A heavy object on a narrow base can still be easy to tip if the line is close to the edge.

43
00:03:55.862 --> 00:04:01.348
A lighter object on a wide base can be more stable.

44
00:04:01.348 --> 00:04:06.834
The key factors are support width, center-of-mass position, and how much margin remains before the line leaves the base.

45
00:04:06.834 --> 00:04:12.320
That is the richer physical picture the module wants the learner to keep.

46
00:04:12.320 --> 00:04:17.806
Keep this takeaway in view: Weight language alone is too weak; stability depends on support geometry and mass placement.

47
00:04:17.806 --> 00:04:23.292
Wider base means more stable.

48
00:04:23.292 --> 00:04:28.778
The support zone grows, which increases the tipping margin.

49
00:04:28.778 --> 00:04:34.264
Higher center of mass means less stable.

50
00:04:34.264 --> 00:04:39.750
Tipping becomes easier when the mass is carried higher.

51
00:04:39.750 --> 00:04:45.839
A common trap is to judge stability by mass alone.

52
00:04:45.839 --> 00:04:51.927
Another is to ignore the margin until the object is already tipping visibly.

53
00:04:51.927 --> 00:04:58.016
The smarter habit is earlier: track where the Balance Core line is and how close it is to the edge of the Footprint Zone.

54
00:04:58.016 --> 00:05:04.104
Stability is about the risk condition before the fall happens, not only about the motion after the fall starts.

55
00:05:04.104 --> 00:05:10.193
Keep this takeaway in view: Good stability judgment comes from the line position and the remaining support margin.

56
00:05:10.193 --> 00:05:16.281
Heavier does not automatically mean more stable.

57
00:05:16.281 --> 00:05:22.370
Check the line location before visible tipping begins.

58
00:05:22.370 --> 00:05:25.893
Check yourself.

59
00:05:25.893 --> 00:05:29.416
What must stay inside the Footprint Zone for stability?

60
00:05:29.416 --> 00:05:32.939
The Balance Core line.

61
00:05:32.939 --> 00:05:36.462
What change usually improves stability more directly: wider base or just saying the object is heavier?

62
00:05:36.462 --> 00:05:39.985
The wider base.

63
00:05:39.985 --> 00:05:43.508
And what does a larger inside margin mean?

64
00:05:43.508 --> 00:05:47.032
More stability before tipping can begin.

65
00:05:47.032 --> 00:05:50.555
Those three answers are the whole lesson core.

66
00:05:50.555 --> 00:05:54.078
Keep this takeaway in view: Support geometry first, weight language second.

67
00:05:54.078 --> 00:05:57.601
Must stay inside means Balance Core line.

68
00:05:57.601 --> 00:06:01.124
This is the weight line through the center of mass.

69
00:06:01.124 --> 00:06:04.647
Direct improvement means wider base.

70
00:06:04.647 --> 00:06:08.170
A larger support zone increases stability margin.

71
00:06:08.170 --> 00:06:14.322
You are ready for the worked example now.

72
00:06:14.322 --> 00:06:20.474
One stack's Balance Core line lands only zero point zero five metres inside the right edge of a narrow base.

73
00:06:20.474 --> 00:06:26.625
On a wider platform, the same load line lands zero point three zero metres inside the edge.

74
00:06:26.625 --> 00:06:32.777
Stability depends on whether the line stays inside the Footprint Zone and how much margin remains.

75
00:06:32.777 --> 00:06:38.929
The wider platform leaves a much larger inside margin, so it is more stable and gives more protection against tipping.

76
00:06:38.929 --> 00:06:45.081
Keep this takeaway in view: The worked example is ready: compare the inside margin, then state the stability result.

77
00:06:45.081 --> 00:06:51.233
Step 1: Check whether the Balance Core line is still inside the base in each setup.

78
00:06:51.233 --> 00:06:57.385
Step 2: Compare how much margin remains before the edge is reached.

79
00:06:57.385 --> 00:07:03.536
narrow base margin = 0.05 m.

80
00:07:03.536 --> 00:07:09.688
wide base margin = 0.30 m.

81
00:07:09.688 --> 00:07:15.840
The bigger safety margin before the edge gives the wider platform the better stability.
